Key-Point Explanation: Why not use Chinese keys? (1) JS engines have poor tolerance for Chinese identifiers (older browsers throw errors); (2) backends, JSON, and URL encoding may introduce mojibake; (3) IDE autocomplete, search, and refactoring tools have inconsistent support for Chinese. Best Practice: Use human-readable Chinese names in CSV, and use unified English variables internally in JS.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"5":{"title":"1.1 Data Generation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ①：字段映射 —— 中文 → 英文
const COLUMN_MAP = {
  "序号": "id",
  "姓名": "name",
  "性别": "gender",
  "生日": "birthday",
  "年龄": "age",
  "班级": "class",
};","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"6":{"title":"1.1 Data Generation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"After CSV loading, all fields are strings; you need to convert the index/age to Number and the birthday to a Date object.

Key-Point Explanation: +d["年龄"] is the unary plus operator, the fastest way in JS to convert a string to a number (shorter than Number() / parseInt()); new Date(str) can parse ISO formats like "2007-08-15", but has poor compatibility with "2007/8/15", so fall back to d3.timeParse("%Y-%m-%d"). Pitfall: Without trim(), the trailing space in "软件工程1班 " will cause later filtering/aggregation to fail.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"7":{"title":"1.1 Data Generation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ②：类型转换函数 —— 用户原文「推荐方式」
function transformRow(d) {
  return {
    id:       +d["序号"],              // 一元 + 把字符串转 Number
    name:     d["姓名"].trim(),       // trim() 去掉首尾空白
    birthday: new Date(d["生日"]), // 字符串 → Date 对象
    age:      +d["年龄"],
    class:    d["班级"].trim(),
  };
}","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"8":{"title":"1.1 Data Generation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: d3.csv is an asynchronous function and must be awaited. The row callback in the second argument is invoked immediately after each row is parsed — which saves memory compared to first await d3.csv(...) then .map(transformRow) (transform while reading). Significance of Asynchronous Loading: It does not block page rendering, so the user can keep interacting.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"9":{"title":"1.1 Data Generation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ③：一行完成加载 + 转换（用户原文「代码示例 1」）
const data = await d3.csv("data/newstudent.csv", transformRow);

// 验证类型确实转换了：
console.log(typeof data[0].age);        // "number"
console.log(data[0].birthday instanceof Date); // true","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"10":{"title":"1.1 Data Generation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"demo","text":"Explains asynchronous loading and field mapping using the new-student dataset (synthesized by DeepSeek)","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-1","query":{"tab-1":"demo"}}},"11":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"DIKW Pyramid — Data → Information → Knowledge → Wisdom","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"12":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"DIKW Pyramid: Data is 'raw data', Information is 'organized data', Knowledge is 'patterns/regularities', and Wisdom is 'judgment/strategy'.
TierTermQuestion AnsweredDataRaw, discrete observations—InformationData that has been processed, organized, and given contextWho / What / When / WhereKnowledgeFurther refinement and understanding of informationHowWisdomApplying knowledge to make wise judgments and strategic decisionsWhy / What to do","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"13":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Key Insight: Visualization mainly lifts Data to Information; in a few scenarios it can reach Knowledge, but it is difficult to cross over to Wisdom.
Data → Information: 'Translate' the table into readable graphics (line chart, bar chart, heatmap)
Information → Knowledge: Pattern discovery requires statistical models (machine learning)
Knowledge → Wisdom: Decisions and strategy are beyond the capability of visualization
Rowley (2007) Critique: DIKW is an oversimplified hierarchical model; in reality the boundary between Data and Information is blurred. But as a teaching scaffold, it remains most valuable.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"14":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"DIKW Pyramid — Data → Information → Knowledge → Wisdom","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"15":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"The pyramid is composed of 4 trapezoids joined together — the d attribute of the D3 path.

Key-Point Explanation: The SVG path commands M (moveto), L (lineto), and Z (close) are the three most commonly used. Template literals `${...}` can embed JS variables directly, making them more readable than string concatenation. Practice: For complex shapes, use D3's d3.symbol() (which has built-in ★●▲ etc.); hand-writing paths is error-prone.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"16":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ①：trapezoid 路径 —— 从上到下 4 个点的连线
const topW = 60, botW = 120;
const yTop = 20, yBot = 100;
const path = `
  M ${cx - topW/2} ${yTop}
  L ${cx + topW/2} ${yTop}
  L ${cx + botW/2} ${yBot}
  L ${cx - botW/2} ${yBot}
  Z
`;","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"17":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: You must use function() instead of arrow functions — because D3 callbacks use this to access the current element, and arrow functions capture the outer this, causing it to fail. This is the most common D3 pitfall. transition().duration() is D3's built-in animation API, even more concise than jQuery.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"18":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ②：选择集绑定事件 —— D3 的事件模型
const g = svg.append("g")
  .style("cursor", "pointer")
  .on("mouseover", function() {       // 注意 function 而非箭头函数
    d3.select(this).select("path")
       .transition().duration(150)
       .attr("fill", d3.color(layer.color).brighter(0.5));
  })
  .on("click", () => showDetail(layer));","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"19":{"title":"1.2 Data Definition","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"demo","text":"DIKW Pyramid — Data → Information → Knowledge → Wisdom
Hover over the pyramid → highlight that tier · click → show that tier's definition / examples / common visualizations on the right","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-2","query":{"tab-2":"demo"}}},"20":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Stevens Measurement Scales × Mackinlay Visual Channel Ranking","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"21":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Your original text gives 3 types of attributes:
Categorical Attribute: a symbol or name with no ordering (gender, name)
Ordinal Attribute: attribute values have an order (degree: bachelor's/master's/doctorate)
Numerical Attribute: quantitative method (age, price)","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"22":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Stevens divides measurement scales into 4 types, finer than your original text:
CategoryAllowed OperationsTypical ExampleMaps to Your OriginalNominalEquivalence / Non-equivalenceName, GenderCategoricalOrdinal+ > <Rank, Class indexOrdinalInterval+ Add/SubtractTemperature (°C)—Ratio+ Add/Subtract/Multiply/DivideAge, PriceNumerical

Key Difference: The interval scale has no 'true zero point' (0°C is not 'no temperature'); the ratio scale has a true zero (0 yuan = no money).","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"23":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Mackinlay proposes dividing visual channels into 3 tiers:
TierChannelSuitable AttributeQuantitativePosition, Length, AngleRatio / IntervalOrderedBrightness, Density, SaturationOrdinalQualitativeHue, ShapeNominal

Core Finding: Position is the strongest quantitative channel; hue is the strongest qualitative channel. Mismatch → information loss.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"24":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Stevens Measurement Scales × Mackinlay Visual Channel Ranking","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"25":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: A D3 scale's domain() is the data domain (input) and range() is the value range (output pixels). Choosing the wrong scale is the most common beginner mistake: using scaleLinear for categorical data produces meaningless intermediate values (e.g., class 0.5). Memory Aid: Use Linear for continuous quantitative, Band for discrete categorical, and Point for ordinal.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"26":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ①：scale 选型 —— 属性  通道的映射
// 比率型数据 → scaleLinear（连续定量）
const x = d3.scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]).range([0, 800]);

// 名义/序数型数据 → scaleBand（离散分类）
const x = d3.scaleBand().domain(["软件1班", "软件2班"]).range([0, 800]);","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"27":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: Viridis is the benchmark sequential colormap — color-vision-deficiency (CVD) friendly and perceptually uniform. Common Mistake: Using the rainbow colormap interpolateRainbow to encode numerical data — it is neither CVD-friendly nor free of false boundaries (blue→green jumps). Class Question: When to use a diverging colormap? Answer: Data that has a 'middle value' (e.g., positive/negative, temperature vs. average).","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"28":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ②：颜色映射 —— 顺序型 vs 定性型 vs 发散型
// 顺序型（顺序有意义）→ interpolateViridis / interpolateBlues
const color = d3.scaleSequential(d3.interpolateViridis);

// 定性型（无顺序）→ schemeTableau10
const color = d3.scaleOrdinal().range(d3.schemeTableau10);

// 发散型（有中点）→ interpolateRdBu
const color = d3.scaleDiverging(d3.interpolateRdBu);","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"29":{"title":"1.3 Data Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"demo","text":"Stevens Measurement Scales × Mackinlay Visual Channel Ranking
4 sub-demos: ① Categorical uses hue (✓) ② Ordinal uses brightness (✓) ③ Numerical uses position (✓) ④ Numerical uses hue (mismatch — cannot tell magnitude)","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-3","query":{"tab-3":"demo"}}},"30":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"D3's 8 Functional Array Operations","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"31":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Your original text lists 8 common functions provided by D3:
map(iterable, mapper) — similar to Array.map
reduce(iterable, reducer, init) — similar to Array.reduce
cross(...iterables, reducer) — Cartesian product
merge(iterables) — merge into a flat array
pairs(iterable, reducer) — adjacent element pairs
transpose(matrix) — matrix transpose
zip(...arrays) — combine by position
filter(iterable, test) — similar to Array.filter","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"32":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Common characteristics of these 8 functions:
Immutable: input unchanged, returns a new array
Pure function: same input → same output, no side effects
Chainable: returns an array, can be followed by the next transformation
Why Functional?
Can be reasoned about: each transformation layer is independent and testable
Parallelizable: map/reduce/filter are naturally parallelizable
Cacheable: outputs of pure functions can be memoized
The Prototype of the Data Cube (OLAP): transpose performs wide/long format conversion, and group + rollup performs slicing/dicing/drilling — this is exactly the core of BI systems.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"33":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"D3's 8 Functional Array Operations","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"34":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: Each step of the chained call returns a new array and does not modify the original array. Compared with Imperative: The imperative style needs 4 temporary variables + a for loop, while the functional style does it in one line. Performance Trade-off: Each map/filter creates a new array, which incurs a performance cost for very large arrays (only significant above the million level).","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"35":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ①：函数式数据流 —— map 后接 filter 再 reduce
const avgAge =
  students
    .filter(s => s.gender === "男")    // ① 过滤男生
    .map(s => s.age)                    // ② 提取年龄
    .reduce((sum, a) => sum + a, 0) // ③ 合计
  / students.filter(s => s.gender === "男").length; // ④ 求平均","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"36":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: The Cartesian product is commonly used in 'pairwise combination' analysis (e.g., counting students by gender × major). The second reducer argument is optional and is used to 'combine' the two elements. Extension: The Cartesian product of 3 arrays yields 8 combinations; for n arrays it is the product of their sizes.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"37":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ②：cross —— 笛卡尔积，用于两两组合
const genders = ["男", "女"];
const majors  = ["软件工程", "数据科学"];
const combos  = d3.cross(genders, majors);
// → [["男","软件工程"], ["男","数据科学"], ["女","软件工程"], ["女","数据科学"]]","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"38":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: group vs rollup is the difference between 'keep groups' and 'aggregate groups'. The second argument of rollup is a reducer function (here it takes the length); in later chapter doc-02, rollup will be used in detail for tree-structured data.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"39":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ③：group 与 rollup —— 层次聚合
// group 返回 Map（保留所有元素）
const byClass = d3.group(students, s => s.class);
// rollup 返回 Map（每个 key 只保留聚合值）
const countByClass = d3.rollup(
  students,
  v => v.length,
  s => s.class
);","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"40":{"title":"1.4 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"demo","text":"D3's 8 Functional Array Operations
Use the dropdown to switch among the 8 functions; the right side shows input/output comparison + a real scenario (new-student data)","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-4","query":{"tab-4":"demo"}}},"41":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Selection and the enter / update / exit Three-State Model","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"explain"}}},"42":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"Your original text outlines 6 selector methods:
MethodReturnsd3.selectsingle elementd3.selectAllselection of multiple elementsselection.selectthe first descendant of each selected elementselection.selectAllall descendantsselection.selectChildthe first direct child elementselection.selectChildrenall direct child elements","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"explain"}}},"43":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"The core of D3 is Data-Join: 'binding' a data array to DOM elements.
When the data changes:
New data > old data: the extras enter enter (need to append)
New data = old data: the existing elements enter update (need attribute changes)
New data < old data: the surplus elements enter exit (need to remove)
three states","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"explain"}}},"44":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"explain","text":"ImperativeDeclarative (Data-Join)createElement then append one by one.data(arr).join(...) in one linefull re-renderincremental update (better performance)element identity lostkey function preserves stable identity (natural animation)disconnected from the data flownaturally fits the functional data flow","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"explain"}}},"45":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Selection and the enter / update / exit Three-State Model","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"46":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: The three callbacks of join are invoked in the enter / update / exit phases respectively. This 'declarative three-state' pattern is more concise than the v3 enter().append() + exit().remove() style, and less error-prone. Analogy: React's diff + patch — the same idea, but React does it for you, while D3 lets you write the transformation functions yourself.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"47":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ①：D3 v5+ 的 join() API —— 优雅处理三态
const circles = svg.selectAll("circle")
  .data(currentData)
  .join(
    enter  => enter.append("circle").attr("fill", "green"),  // enter
    update => update.attr("fill", "cyan"),                              // update
    exit   => exit.remove()                                                   // exit
  );","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"48":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: The key function is the soul of D3 animation. Without a key, D3 binds identity by index: reordering data causes 'all elements to move'. With a key, each circle knows its id, and on reorder only cx changes, so the transition animation is natural. Analogy: Exactly the same idea as React's key={item.id}.","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"49":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ②：key 函数 —— 元素身份的稳定器
//  不带 key：数据顺序变化 → 所有元素都"update"，过渡动画错乱
svg.selectAll("circle").data(newData);

//  带 key：用唯一字段绑定身份，元素"知道自己是哪个"
svg.selectAll("circle").data(newData, d => d.id);

// 同样的逻辑用 React 描述：key={item.id}","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"50":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"Key-Point Explanation: select is used to 'operate on a single element', and selectAll is used to 'bind data in bulk'. Common Mistake: When using selectAll(".bar") to bind data to an array, the number of elements must match the number of data items, otherwise it will 'leave gaps' or 'overflow'. Best Practice: First use selectAll to create placeholder elements (possibly empty), then .data().join(...).","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"51":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"code","text":"// 关键点 ③：选择器的核心区别
// select：返回 1 个元素（第一个匹配）；数据绑定只对这一个元素
const title = d3.select("#title");

// selectAll：返回多个元素；数据绑定按顺序一一对应
const bars = d3.selectAll(".bar").data(values);","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"52":{"title":"1.5 D3 Selectors","breadcrumb":"Ch 1 · Data","kind":"demo","text":"Selection and the enter / update / exit Three-State Model
Click the button to trigger enter (green) / update (blue) / exit (red), and observe the three-state switching","target":{"path":"/lessons/01","hash":"#ch-1-5","query":{"tab-5":"demo"}}},"53":{"title":"2.1 Visualization Workflow","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"The complete pipeline from "getting a table" to "drawing a chart"","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"54":{"title":"2.1 Visualization Workflow","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"3 visualization pipeline models: ① Reference the Card 1999 model for box flowcharts → ② Mackinlay's automation principles → ③ van Wijk's model (with interaction feedback).
The original image is your concept map. This lesson uses D3 to redraw this concept map — every node expands details when clicked.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"55":{"title":"2.1 Visualization Workflow","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"ModelAuthor / YearCore IdeaCard Data Flow ModelCard, Mackinlay & Shneiderman, 1999Raw data → data table → visual structure → view (nested transformations D→T→V→C/L/S)Mackinlay's Automation PrinciplesMackinlay, 1986For each data type, automatically pick the visual channel with the highest “quantitative accuracy” (e.g., quantitative → position, categorical → hue)van Wijk Feedback Modelvan Wijk, 2005View Filter Param Other Views — emphasizing the interaction feedback loop

Core teaching points: the three models describe different facets of the same thing — Card gives you the “pipeline,” Mackinlay gives you “automated decisions,” and van Wijk gives you the “feedback loop.” They layer on top of each other rather than conflict.
Card model: tells you “after transform comes mapping, after mapping comes view”
Mackinlay: tells you “how to automate the mapping step”
van Wijk: tells you “after view, you still need to feed user actions back”","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"56":{"title":"2.1 Visualization Workflow","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"The complete pipeline from "getting a table" to "drawing a chart"","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"57":{"title":"2.1 Visualization Workflow","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"5 step nodes arranged horizontally, connected by SVG-marker-drawn arrows between adjacent nodes.

Key explanation: marker-end="url(#arrowhead)" is SVG's referencing mechanism — it “embeds” a shape defined inside <defs> at the line's end; classed("selected", predicate) takes a boolean function and toggles the “selected” state of every node in one call, so you avoid writing forEach yourself.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"58":{"title":"2.1 Visualization Workflow","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"// 1. 用 <defs> + <marker> 定义箭头
svg.append("defs").append("marker")
   .attr("id", "arrowhead")
   .attr("viewBox", "0 -5 10 10")
   .attr("refX", 8)            // 箭头位置校准
   .attr("markerWidth", 6)
   .attr("orient", "auto")       // 跟随线方向旋转
   .append("path").attr("d", "M0,-5L10,0L0,5").attr("fill", "#94a3b8");

 // 2. 用 marker-end 引用箭头（重要）
svg.append("line")
   .attr("marker-end", "url(#arrowhead)")
   .attr("x1", x1).attr("y1", y).attr("x2", x2).attr("y2", y);

 // 3. 节点  数据 绑定：d3.data + click 事件
svg.selectAll(".node").data(nodes)
   .join("g")
   .on("click", (event, d) => {
     //  关键：classed 是 D3 状态管理核心 API
     svg.selectAll(".node").classed("selected", n => n.id === d.id);
     document.getElementById("d-4-1-detail").innerHTML = d.detail;
   });","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"59":{"title":"2.1 Visualization Workflow","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"demo","text":"The complete pipeline from "getting a table" to "drawing a chart"","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-1","query":{"tab-1":"demo"}}},"60":{"title":"2.1 Visualization Workflow","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"demo","text":"Click any node to see its mapping in the Card / Mackinlay / van Wijk models","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-1","query":{"tab-1":"demo"}}},"61":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"From “group by major” to “group by dimension combinations”","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"62":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"The Card 1999 data-flow model splits visualization into four stages: raw data → (transform) → data table → (map) → visual structure → (render) → view. Section 2.2 is the first arrow, “raw data → data table” — i.e. Data Transformation.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"63":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"Use the freshman data to do rollup bucketed aggregation by “major / class”. The core of your demo is d3.rollup's chained calls — one function that performs multi-dimensional grouping.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"64":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"Going from “raw data” to a “data table,” the D layer performs four kinds of operations:
OperationWhat it doesD3 implementationClean / filterRemove missing/outliers; keep rows matching a condition.filter(), d3.csv(row) callbackAggregateGroup by key, reduce each group to a single valued3.rollup(), d3.group()JoinCombine two tables by key (e.g., student table joined to class table)d3.map + manual indexingDeriveCompute a new column from existing ones (e.g., extract “major” from “class”)compute a new field in the row callback

The rollup aggregation demonstrated here belongs to the “aggregate” row. But reading this table, you realize data transformation is the “first kilometer” of the whole pipeline — if the data table isn't clean, all the mapping and rendering downstream is wasted.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"65":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"d3.rollup is a functional composition of “bucket by key, then reduce once”:
It returns a Map (not an array, not an object), so:
Iterate using for ([k, v] of m) or m.entries()
Nested rollup produces a nested Map
Difference from d3.group: rollup reduces the in-group data to a single value, while group preserves the original array","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"66":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"const result = d3.rollup(
  data,                    // 输入数组
  v => v.length,          // reducer
  d => d.major,           // key1（外层）
  d => d.class           // key2（内层，可选）
);","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"67":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"In 1993 Codd proposed 12 OLAP rules; three of their core operations can be simulated with rollup:
Slice: fix one dimension (e.g., only look at the 2025 cohort) → equivalent to .filter() then rollup
Dice: group by two dimensions → equivalent to a double-key rollup
Drill: drill down level by level → equivalent to nested rollup + recursive expansion
Classroom Takeaway: a visualization system's “drill-down interaction” is typically backed by a combination of rollup + hierarchy. This lesson demonstrates d3.rollup's multi-level capability.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"68":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"From “group by major” to “group by dimension combinations”","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"69":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"Two key layers: outer = major, inner = class, reducer = count.

Key explanation: The key parameter of d3.rollup is a rest parameter (...keys), so passing 1 → single-layer Map, 2 → nested Map, 3 → 3-layer nesting. This is OLAP's “dimension combination.”","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"70":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"// 双层 rollup：返回嵌套 Map，外层 key=专业，内层 key=班级
const nested = d3.rollup(
  students,
  v => v.length,           // 每组聚合为单一值（这里是 count）
  d => d.major,            // 外层 key
  d => d.class             // 内层 key（rest 参数，可继续加）
);

// 遍历嵌套 Map：
for (const [major, inner] of nested) {
  for (const [cls, count] of inner) {
    console.log(major, cls, count);
  }
}","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"71":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"The CSV only has a “class” field (e.g., “Software Engineering Class 1”), so a regex is needed to split out the “major” and the “class number.”

Key explanation: In the regex /^(.+?)(\d+班)$/, the .+? is non-greedy matching — it keeps the middle as short as possible and leaves the tail for \d+班. Without non-greedy: .+ would consume the whole string, leaving nothing, and m[1] would be undefined. This is a regex pitfall students frequently step on; you must master it.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"72":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"// d3.csv 第二参 row 回调里逐行 transform
const data = await d3.csv("./data/newstudent.csv", d => {
  const cls = d["班级"].trim();
  const m = cls.match(/^(.+?)(\d+班)$/);  // 中间非贪婪 + 末尾"数字+班"
  return {
    id:    +d["序号"],
    name:  d["姓名"].trim(),
    class: cls,
    major: m ? m[1] : cls,  // "软件工程1班" → "软件工程"
  };
});","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"73":{"title":"2.2 Data Transformation: Raw → Data Table","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"demo","text":"From “group by major” to “group by dimension combinations”","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-2","query":{"tab-2":"demo"}}},"74":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"The algorithmic core of hierarchical data → screen coordinates","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"75":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"Following the 2.2 data transformation, we now have a “data table.” Section 2.3 is the second arrow, “data table → visual structure” — i.e. Visual Mapping. It answers one core question: for each data attribute, which visual channel should express it?","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"76":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"Your Section 4.2 mentioned “tree-structure visualization.” This lesson expands on the tree layout algorithm — once you have hierarchical data, how do you place nodes on screen without overlap?","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"77":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"A visual channel is the carrier that “encodes data onto a visual element”: position, length, area, angle, color, shape, texture… Mackinlay (1986) gave the classic ranking: for different data types, the quantitative accuracy of each channel differs. That is the figure below.
Mackinlay 1986 visual channel ranking
Figure 1 · Mackinlay (1986) channel ranking: for Quantitative / Ordinal / Nominal data, the quantitative-accuracy ordering of each channel. Position is always the most accurate.
Position: ranks #1 for all three types — the eye is most sensitive to position
Quantitative data: length → angle → slope → area (bar charts beat area charts)
Nominal data: position → hue → texture → connection → shape","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"78":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"The visual structure is built on marks: Point, Line, Area. These marks are then “marked up” through channels: position, color, shape, tilt, size. The next two figures break this apart.
Marks: Points / Lines / Areas
Figure 2 · Three families of marks: Points, Lines, Areas. Marks determine “what a datum looks like” — the skeleton of the visual structure.
Visual channels: Position / Color / Shape / Tilt / Size
Figure 3 · Common visual-channel families: Position (horizontal / vertical / both), Color, Shape, Tilt, Size (length / area / volume).","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"79":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"By “whether it can express magnitude,” channels split into two classes: Magnitude channels express ordered / quantitative attributes; Identity channels express categorical attributes. Picking the wrong class misleads readers.
Magnitude Channels: Ordered Attributes
Figure 4 · Magnitude Channels: express “ordered attributes.” Ordered by accuracy — position on a common scale is best; length / tilt / area next; color luminance, saturation, and volume are weakest (all marked “Same” — ambiguous ordering).
Identity Channels: Categorical Attributes
Figure 5 · Identity Channels: express “categorical attributes” — spatial region, hue, motion, shape. They distinguish different categories but cannot rank “more vs less.”
Where this lesson lands: tree layout (Reingold-Tilford) is essentially a visual mapping — it uses the position channel to encode hierarchy as “parent above, children below.” The algorithm follows.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"80":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"The algorithm proposed by Reingold & Tilford in 1981 is still the implementation core of d3.tree(). Core idea:
First pass (bottom-up): for each parent node, compute how much horizontal space its left and right subtrees “occupy”
Second pass (top-down): assign every node an absolute x-coordinate (so siblings hug the parent's centerline without overlapping)
Third pass: handle “threads” — fix the alignment when children cross the parent's centerline
Complexity handling:
O(n) time — faster than a naive “recursive halving”
A node's minimum left/right subtree width = the horizontal space needed at minimum to hold all its children
Layout-strategy options: d3.tree (left-right), d3.cluster (left-right, packed), d3.treemap (area)","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"81":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"hierarchy(data): convert any nested data into a hierarchy structure (each node gets parent / children / depth)
tree().size([h, w]): Reingold-Tilford algorithm, left-right layout
cluster().size([h, w]): align all leaf nodes to the same depth
treemap().tile(...): area-based (slice-and-dice / squarified / resquarify algorithms)
pack().radius(d => ...): circle packing
partition().size([h, w]): icicle / sunburst layout","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"82":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"The algorithmic core of hierarchical data → screen coordinates","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"83":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"This is the standard d3-hierarchy pattern: first build the hierarchy, then compute the tree layout, and finally draw links + nodes.

Key explanation: treeData.x is the depth direction (vertical by default), while treeData.y is the horizontal direction (different nodes at the same depth). So linkHorizontal uses x=d.y, y=d.x — the horizontal direction rides on y, and the vertical direction rides on x. This “x-axis rides on y, y-axis rides on x” swap is unique to horizontal trees — commit it to memory.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"84":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"// 1. hierarchy：从嵌套 JSON → 计算每个节点的 parent/children/depth
const root = d3.hierarchy(rootData);

 // 2. tree layout：给每个节点算 x/y 坐标
const treeLayout = d3.tree().size([360, 720]);
const treeData = treeLayout(root);

 // 3. 画 links：父-子连线（Reingold-Tilford 算法结果就在这里）
g.selectAll(".link").data(treeData.links()).join("path")
  .attr("d", d3.linkHorizontal().x(d => d.y).y(d => d.x));

 // 4. 画 nodes：圆 + 文字
const ng = g.selectAll(".node").data(treeData.descendants()).join("g")
  .attr("transform", d => `translate(${d.y}, ${d.x})`);

ng.append("circle").attr("r", 5);
ng.append("text")
  .attr("text-anchor", d => d.children ? "end" : "start")
  .attr("x", d => d.children ? -8 : 8)
  .text(d => d.data.name);","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"85":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"separation accepts a function and returns the “spacing multiplier” between [node, parent].

Key explanation: The default value of separation is (a, b) => a.parent === b.parent ? 1 : 2. Change it to 0.5 → everything squishes together; change it to 2.0 → the tree widens. There is a slider in the demo so you can adjust it in real time and see the effect.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"86":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"const treeLayout = d3.tree()
  .size([360, 720])
  .separation((a, b) => (a.parent === b.parent ? 1 : 2));
                              //   ↑ 兄弟节点紧贴        ↑ 非兄弟至少隔 2 倍","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"87":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"demo","text":"The algorithmic core of hierarchical data → screen coordinates","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"demo"}}},"88":{"title":"2.3 Visual Mapping: Data → Visual Structure","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"demo","text":"Switch aggregation levels (major → class / gender → major → class, etc.) and drag the slider to adjust separation · this is visual mapping via the “position channel”","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-3","query":{"tab-3":"demo"}}},"89":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"Render the tree as a collapsible HTML list — handling three pitfalls: click, bubbling, and delegation","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"90":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"Following the 2.3 visual mapping, we now have a “visual structure.” Section 2.4 covers the last two arrows: “visual structure → view” (rendering) and “view user” (interaction feedback). Card's V layer and van Wijk's feedback loop both land here.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"91":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"The original document used classed to control node styles and stopPropagation to block bubbling. The focus is on turning the D3 tree layout output into an interactive HTML list.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"92":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"The browser's event flow has three phases (per the DOM spec):
Capturing: window → document → ... → target element (outside to inside)
Target: the target element itself
Bubbling: target element → ... → document → window (inside to outside)
By default, listeners are registered on the bubbling phase. Inside a listener you can call e.stopPropagation() to cut off bubbling so ancestor listeners no longer fire.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"93":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"Animation (transition) isn't “fancy” — it's the time dimension of view rendering. In D3 you use .transition().duration(ms) to smoothly interpolate an attribute from its old value to a new one. The pedagogical value lies in the single duration parameter:
duration=0: instant jump — you can't “see the change happen”
duration=500: a 0.5 s transition — you see the expand / collapse / move smoothly
duration>1500: too slow — students start to drift
This lesson's interactive demo adds a duration slider so you can change this parameter live and see “how rendering unfolds over time.” It's the most direct lesson on “view rendering + interaction feedback.”","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"94":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"Problem: if a list has 1000 li elements and you bind a click listener to each → it's slow, memory-heavy, and newly added elements have to be re-bound.
Solution: bind only 1 click listener on the parent element (ul / div) and lean on bubbling — a click on any child eventually triggers the parent's listener. Use e.target.closest('.label') to figure out “which child element was actually clicked.”
Advantage: 1 listener replaces N, and any future newly added elements are automatically supported.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"95":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"explain","text":"selection.classed("selected", true);          // 1) 添加 class
selection.classed("selected", false);         // 2) 移除 class
selection.classed("selected", d => d.id === pickId); // 3) 函数式批量切换","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"96":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"Render the tree as a collapsible HTML list — handling three pitfalls: click, bubbling, and delegation","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"97":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"Use recursive buildList to construct the collapsible tree; use the click on liSel for event delegation rather than binding to each label.

Key explanation: This is the classic pattern of “stopping the toggle button from bubbling up to the li's select listener.” Without stopPropagation, clicking the toggle would simultaneously trigger “toggle collapse” and “select the node,” which would confuse users. This is a core teaching point about the DOM event mechanism.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"98":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"// 父 ul 上绑一次 click，所有子 li 共享
function buildList(liSel, d) {
  const label = liSel.append("span").attr("class", "label");

  label.on("click.toggle", (event) => {
    //  关键 ①：阻止冒泡 —— toggle 按钮不该触发"选中"
    event.stopPropagation();
    d.data._collapsed = !d.data._collapsed;
    render();
  });

  liSel.on("click.select", (event) => {
    //  关键 ②：如果 click 来自 toggle，已经被 stopPropagation 阻断，不会到这里
    document.querySelectorAll("li").forEach(li => li.classList.remove("selected"));
    liSel.classed("selected", true);
  });
}","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"99":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"Recursive rendering with d3 selections: every li contains another ul/li, then buildList recurses into it.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"100":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"function render() {
  $tree.innerHTML = "";
  const ul = d3.create("ul");
  ul.selectAll("li").data([root]).join("li")
    .each(function(d) { buildList(d3.select(this), d); });
  $tree.appendChild(ul.node());
}","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"101":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"The “time dimension” of view rendering: smoothly interpolate node expand / collapse instead of an instant jump. duration is controlled by a live slider.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"102":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"// 关键：transition + duration = 让属性随时间平滑变化
svg.selectAll(".node")
  .transition()                                  // 进入过渡状态
  .duration(DURATION)                          // 时长（ms），滑块控制
  .attr("transform", d => `translate(${d.y}, ${d.x})`)
  .attr("opacity", 1);

// 新节点进入时：先设 opacity=0，再过渡到 1 → 淡入效果
ng.attr("opacity", 0)
  .transition().duration(DURATION).attr("opacity", 1);","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"103":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"Key explanation: Why use innerHTML = "" to clear and redraw? D3 has no “smart-update” API for tree structures (where hierarchy changes) — expanding or collapsing a node changes the parent-child structure, and the simplest approach is a full re-render. Use selection.each() together with d3.select(this) to convert the raw element back into a selection — this is the key pattern for D3-native-DOM interop.","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"104":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"code","text":"<!-- HTML 滑块：监听 input 事件即可改 DURATION -->
<input id="dur" type="range" min="0" max="1500" step="50" value="500">

// JS：改变 DURATION 后重放动画
document.getElementById("dur").addEventListener("input", e => {
  DURATION = +e.target.value;
  render();   // 用新时长重新做一次过渡
});","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"105":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"demo","text":"Render the tree as a collapsible HTML list — handling three pitfalls: click, bubbling, and delegation","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"demo"}}},"106":{"title":"2.4 View Rendering & User Interaction","breadcrumb":"Ch 2 · Viz Workflow","kind":"demo","text":"Click the triangle to toggle collapse / expand · click an item to select it (see the log) · drag the “animation duration” slider to see the transition","target":{"path":"/lessons/02","hash":"#ch-2-4","query":{"tab-4":"demo"}}},"107":{"title":"3.1 Color Model Basics","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"explain","text":"Why do we need 4 color spaces? What is each one good at? What does the world look like through the eyes of someone with color blindness?","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"108":{"title":"3.1 Color Model Basics","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"explain","text":"When designing a colormap, do not interpolate directly in RGB—because RGB is not perceptually uniform (255,0,0 and 0,255,0 "look" very different in lightness). You need a perceptually uniform color space (Lab / HCL) to interpolate and get smooth gradients.","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"109":{"title":"3.1 Color Model Basics","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"explain","text":"RGB: additive model, device-dependent. The default for screen display.
HSL: artist-friendly (independent hue / saturation / lightness), but still not perceptually uniform.
CIE Lab: perceptually uniform, the gold standard for designing colormaps.
CMYK: subtractive (print). Not used in web visualizations.","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"110":{"title":"3.1 Color Model Basics","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"explain","text":"About 8% of men and 0.5% of women are color-blind. Three common types:
Protanopia (red-blind): ~1% of men
Deuteranopia (green-blind): ~6% of men (most common)
Tritanopia (blue-blind): extremely rare","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"111":{"title":"3.1 Color Model Basics","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"code","text":"Why do we need 4 color spaces? What is each one good at? What does the world look like through the eyes of someone with color blindness?","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"112":{"title":"3.1 Color Model Basics","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"code","text":"Key point 1: Hand-written RGB HSL
Writing RGBHSL yourself is to understand the physical meaning of each step—"the largest channel determines hue, the difference determines saturation"—which is more illuminating than using a d3 library for understanding why hue cycles between 0–360°.

Key point 2: CVD simulation matrix
Key point 3: d3's built-in multi-space support","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"113":{"title":"3.1 Color Model Basics","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"code","text":"function rgbToHsl(r, g, b) {
  r /= 255; g /= 255; b /= 255;
  const max = Math.max(r, g, b), min = Math.min(r, g, b);
  let h, s, l = (max + min) / 2;
  if (max === min) { h = s = 0; }
  else {
    const d = max - min;
    s = l > 0.5 ? d / (2 - max - min) : d / (max + min);
    switch (max) {
      case r: h = (g - b) / d + (g < b ? 6 : 0); break;
      case g: h = (b - r) / d + 2; break;
      case b: h = (r - g) / d + 4; break;
    }
    h /= 6;
  }
  return [h * 360, s * 100, l * 100];
}

const CVD_MATRICES = {
  deuteranopia: [
    [0.625, 0.375, 0.000],
    [0.700, 0.300, 0.000],
    [0.000, 0.300, 0.700]
  ],
  protanopia:   [[0.567, 0.433, 0.000], [0.558, 0.442, 0.000], [0.000, 0.242, 0.758]],
  tritanopia:   [[0.950, 0.050, 0.000], [0.000, 0.433, 0.567], [0.000, 0.475, 0.525]]
};

function simulateCVD([r, g, b], type) {
  const m = CVD_MATRICES[type];
  return [
    r * m[0][0] + g * m[0][1] + b * m[0][2],
    r * m[1][0] + g * m[1][1] + b * m[1][2],
    r * m[2][0] + g * m[2][1] + b * m[2][2]
  ].map(v => Math.round(Math.max(0, Math.min(255, v))));
}

const c = d3.color('rgb(100, 50, 200)');
c.rgb();   // {r, g, b}
c.hsl();   // {h, s, l}
c.lab();   // {l, a, b}  ← 感知均匀
c.hcl();   // {h, c, l}  ← Lab 极坐标版","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"114":{"title":"3.1 Color Model Basics","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"demo","text":"Why do we need 4 color spaces? What is each one good at? What does the world look like through the eyes of someone with color blindness?","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-1","query":{"tab-1":"demo"}}},"115":{"title":"3.2 Colormap: 3 Types","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"explain","text":"Sequential / Diverging / Qualitative. Choosing the wrong type = misleading your readers.","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"116":{"title":"3.2 Colormap: 3 Types","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"explain","text":"Sequential: data with a natural order (population, temperature). Monotonic lightness + monotonic hue. Examples: Viridis / Plasma / Cividis
Diverging: data with a meaningful "midpoint" (e.g., zero, signed deviation, white median). Different hues at the two ends, white/light in the middle. Examples: RdBu / BrBG / PiYG
Qualitative: unordered data (e.g., product categories). Distinct hues, similar lightness. Examples: Tableau10 / Set1 / Set3","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"117":{"title":"3.2 Colormap: 3 Types","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"explain","text":"Using the diverging palette RdBu for "murder rates in 50 states" (ordinal data)—the middle band looks near-white, so states with a murder rate "around 100" appear to be the "midpoint"—but 100 has no semantic meaning, so this is purely misleading.","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"118":{"title":"3.2 Colormap: 3 Types","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"explain","text":"Viridis / Cividis: excellent (recommended by Nature Methods)
