Use DOMParser to parse SVG strings, then walk all fill attributes and apply the CVD matrix. Safer than hand-concatenating SVG strings.","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-8","query":{"tab-8":"code"}}},"168":{"title":"3.8 CVD Paper Figure Test","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"code","text":"function cvdTransformSvg(svgString, type) {
  const doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(svgString, 'image/svg+xml');
  doc.querySelectorAll('[fill]').forEach(el => {
    const c = d3.color(el.getAttribute('fill'));
    if (!c) return;
    const rgb = [Math.round(c.rgb().r), Math.round(c.rgb().g), Math.round(c.rgb().b)];
    const sim = simulateCVD(rgb, type);
    el.setAttribute('fill', `rgb(${sim.join(',')})`);
  });
  return new XMLSerializer().serializeToString(doc);
}","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-8","query":{"tab-8":"code"}}},"169":{"title":"3.8 CVD Paper Figure Test","breadcrumb":"Ch 3 · Color","kind":"demo","text":"Can your figure still be understood by someone with CVD? Test it with real top-journal figures.","target":{"path":"/lessons/03","hash":"#ch-3-8","query":{"tab-8":"demo"}}},"170":{"title":"4.1 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Your Crime data is in wide format, but visualization often needs long format. A one-liner conversion function handles it.
本节使用了 colormap（Viridis）画热图。colormap 理论详见 第 3 章 3.2 colormap 三类选择。","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"171":{"title":"4.1 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Section 5.1 used 1985 U.S. crime data (CSV) for the 50 states, with 8 crime-indicator columns.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"172":{"title":"4.1 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Long format (long): one observation per row, columns = fields (e.g., {state, metric, value}). Pros: easy to filter, group, and bind to a d3 join.
Wide format (wide): one entity per row, columns = metrics (e.g., {state, murder, robbery, ...}). Pros: human-readable and intuitive.
Conversion: flatMap + iterating over key names = a few lines of code.
d3.autoType: auto-infers column types (number, date, boolean), saving you from manual +d.field.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"173":{"title":"4.1 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"Your Crime data is in wide format, but visualization often needs long format. A one-liner conversion function handles it.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"174":{"title":"4.1 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"★ Key Point 1: the second argument of d3.csv = the type-conversion callback
★ d3.autoType internally walks each column using Number() / Date() / string detection. Recognized types are converted; unrecognized values stay as strings.

★ Key Point 2: wide → long (the flatMap pattern)
★ flatMap is ES2019, shorthand for “map + one-level flatten”. More intuitive than the map().flat() chain.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"175":{"title":"4.1 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"//  自动类型推断（数字/日期/布尔）
const raw = await d3.csv('./data/crime.csv', d3.autoType);
console.log(raw[0]);  // {state: 'Alabama', population: 3979000, murder: 350, ...}

//  flatMap：每行展开成多个观测
const long = raw.flatMap(d =>
  ['murder', 'robbery', 'burglary'].map(key => ({
    state:  d.state,
    metric: key,
    value:  d[key]
  }))
);
// 现在可以做：d3.group(long, d => d.metric)","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"176":{"title":"4.1 Data Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"demo","text":"Your Crime data is in wide format, but visualization often needs long format. A one-liner conversion function handles it.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-1","query":{"tab-1":"demo"}}},"177":{"title":"4.2 Axis Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Data space → coordinate system space → screen space. Three layers with clearly separated responsibilities; mix them up and everything falls apart.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"178":{"title":"4.2 Axis Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Section 5.1.2 used a scatter plot to display Crime data (murder vs. robbery), with the 1985 data on the coordinate axes.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"179":{"title":"4.2 Axis Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Three-layer space:
Data Space: raw values (e.g., population 27058000, murder rate 8)
Coordinate Space: normalized to 0..innerWidth (standardized coordinates)
Screen Space: add the margin offset (final pixel positions)
Why layer them: elements inside the coordinate system space (lines, points) can be shared — no recalculation when the screen size changes. d3.axisBottom handles the coordinate-system → screen step automatically.
Scale selection:
scaleLinear: uniform distribution, most common
scaleLog: spans orders of magnitude (population from 530k to 27M)
scaleSqrt: area-aware (maps circle area to value)
scaleBand: categorical axis (bar charts)","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"180":{"title":"4.2 Axis Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"Data space → coordinate system space → screen space. Three layers with clearly separated responsibilities; mix them up and everything falls apart.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"181":{"title":"4.2 Axis Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"★ Key Point 1: data space vs. screen space — two layers of scales
★ Misalignment example: writing domain=max and range=[M.l, M.l+innerW] in one scale → changing the margin means editing two places. The three-layer separation keeps things clean.

★ Key Point 2: log scale spans orders of magnitude
★ A log axis is especially well-suited for “exponential growth” data. Human vision is “multiplicative” rather than “additive”, so log turns a “geometric sequence” into “equally-spaced” points on screen.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"182":{"title":"4.2 Axis Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"// 数据空间：值 → 坐标系
const xData = d3.scaleLinear()
  .domain([0, d3.max(data, d => d.x)])
  .range([0, innerW]);

// 屏幕空间：坐标系 → 屏幕（加 margin）
const xScreen = d3.scaleLinear()
  .domain([0, innerW])
  .range([M.l, M.l + innerW]);

// 渲染：数据 → 坐标系 → 屏幕
.attr('cx', d => xScreen(xData(d.x)))

// 50 州人口从 53 万到 2700 万（差 50 倍）
// linear 缩放下，小州挤成一团，CA/NY/TX 占满屏幕
const xLog = d3.scaleLog()
  .domain([1, d3.max(data, d => d.x)])
  .range([0, innerW]);","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"183":{"title":"4.2 Axis Transformation","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"demo","text":"Data space → coordinate system space → screen space. Three layers with clearly separated responsibilities; mix them up and everything falls apart.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-2","query":{"tab-2":"demo"}}},"184":{"title":"4.3 Curve Fitting","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Same 50 points, 10 different line.curve styles. Which to use when?","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"185":{"title":"4.3 Curve Fitting","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Section 5.1.3 used a line + scatter plot to show trends. The original code used curveCatmullRom without explaining why.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"186":{"title":"4.3 Curve Fitting","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Interpolation vs. Fitting:
Interpolation: the curve passes through every data point (e.g., Catmull-Rom, Natural)
Fitting: the curve does not necessarily pass through data points, only reflects the trend (e.g., linear regression, LOESS)
d3.line().curve() offers 10+ curves; the most commonly used are:
step family: for discrete steps (digital signals, state transitions)
catmullRom / natural: tension splines that pass through all points — “looks smooth”
monotoneX: preserves the monotonicity of the original data sequence (no spurious oscillations)
Selection principles: ordered data → monotoneX / linear; jittery data → catmullRom / basis to smooth; discrete states → step","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-3","query":{"tab-3":"explain"}}},"187":{"title":"4.3 Curve Fitting","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"Same 50 points, 10 different line.curve styles. Which to use when?","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"188":{"title":"4.3 Curve Fitting","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"★ Key Point 1: switch curves with d3.line + curve in one line
★ datum(data) passes the array as a whole to the path (not one element at a time). Inside, d3.line loops to generate M/L/C commands.

★ Key Point 2: the trade-off between fitting and interpolation
★ Rule of thumb: more than 30 points → use fitting; fewer than 10 points → use interpolation (preserve full information). Crime data has 50 states → fitting is more robust.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"189":{"title":"4.3 Curve Fitting","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"const line = d3.line()
  .x(d => xScale(d.x))
  .y(d => yScale(d.y))
  .curve(d3.curveCatmullRom);     // <- 这一行换 curve 类型

svg.append('path').datum(data).attr('d', line);

// 拟合：d3.regressionLinear / d3.regressionLoess
import { regressionLinear } from 'd3-regression';
const lin = regressionLinear()
  .x(d => d.population)
  .y(d => d.murder)
  .domain([0, d3.max(data, d => d.population)]);
const points = lin(data);  // 起点-终点
svg.append('path').attr('d', d3.line()(points));","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-3","query":{"tab-3":"code"}}},"190":{"title":"4.3 Curve Fitting","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"demo","text":"Same 50 points, 10 different line.curve styles. Which to use when?","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-3","query":{"tab-3":"demo"}}},"191":{"title":"4.4 Slope Perception (45° Sweet Spot)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Human perception of slope is not linear. 45° is the visual “midpoint”.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"192":{"title":"4.4 Slope Perception (45° Sweet Spot)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Section 5.1.4 mentions that “the optimal average slope is 45°” without explaining why.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"193":{"title":"4.4 Slope Perception (45° Sweet Spot)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Cleveland 1984 experiment: subjects were asked to estimate line slopes:
Slope = 0° or 90°: estimation error is largest
Slope = 45°: estimation is most accurate
The error curve is U-shaped
Stevens’ power law: perceived slope ≈ (actual slope)1.0–1.3. In other words, the eye exaggerates “steepness” by about 30%.
Design principles:
Key data slopes are most stable in the 30°–60° range
When comparing growth curves, use a log axis so steepness lands near 45°
Avoid trend comparisons below 5° or above 85° (errors grow too large)","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-4","query":{"tab-4":"explain"}}},"194":{"title":"4.4 Slope Perception (45° Sweet Spot)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"Human perception of slope is not linear. 45° is the visual “midpoint”.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"195":{"title":"4.4 Slope Perception (45° Sweet Spot)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"★ Key Point 1: convert polar to Cartesian coordinates
★ SVG’s y axis points downward (opposite to math convention), so the sine term must be negated. This is the most common pitfall for beginners.

★ Key Point 2: turn abstract theory into tunable parameters","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"196":{"title":"4.4 Slope Perception (45° Sweet Spot)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"// 角度 → 屏幕坐标
function lineAtAngle(angleDeg, length = 240, origin = {x: 100, y: 240}) {
  const r = (angleDeg * Math.PI) / 180;
  return {
    x1: origin.x,
    y1: origin.y,
    x2: origin.x + length * Math.cos(r),
    y2: origin.y - length * Math.sin(r)   // SVG y 向下为正，所以取负
  };
}

// 让用户拖滑块改角度，立刻看到"哪段斜率最稳"
const slider = document.getElementById('angle');
slider.addEventListener('input', e => {
  const angle = +e.target.value;
  renderLine(angle);     // 30°~60° 误差小，0°/90° 误差大
});","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-4","query":{"tab-4":"code"}}},"197":{"title":"4.4 Slope Perception (45° Sweet Spot)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"demo","text":"Human perception of slope is not linear. 45° is the visual “midpoint”.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-4","query":{"tab-4":"demo"}}},"198":{"title":"4.5 Marching Squares (Contour Lines)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Given a scalar field + threshold, trace the curve where value = threshold.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-5","query":{"tab-5":"explain"}}},"199":{"title":"4.5 Marching Squares (Contour Lines)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"Section 5.2.2 used Marching Squares to extract isocontours from Crime data. The original code was a screenshot placeholder.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-5","query":{"tab-5":"explain"}}},"200":{"title":"4.5 Marching Squares (Contour Lines)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"explain","text":"How Marching Squares works: discretize the scalar field into a grid. For each cell, the “above threshold / not” state of its 4 corners yields a case index from 0 to 15 (24 = 16 cases). Each case corresponds to 0, 1, or 2 line segments that separate the “above-threshold” region from the “not” region.
16 cases: encoded as bit0=BL, bit1=BR, bit2=TR, bit3=TL.
Extension to 3D: Marching Cubes (Lorensen & Cline, 1987), with 28 = 256 cases, widely used for extracting isosurfaces from medical MRI / CT scans.
Application scenarios:
Terrain contour lines (maps)
Medical imaging (MRI / CT isosurfaces)
Density contours (kernel density estimation)
Weather forecasting (isotherms, isobars)","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-5","query":{"tab-5":"explain"}}},"201":{"title":"4.5 Marching Squares (Contour Lines)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"Given a scalar field + threshold, trace the curve where value = threshold.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"202":{"title":"4.5 Marching Squares (Contour Lines)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"★ Key Point 1: the 16-case lookup table (core data structure)
★ The saddle case (5 and 10) is the only ambiguous situation in Marching Squares that requires “checking the center value” — the other 14 cases are resolved with a simple lookup. For saddle cases, compare the cell center to the threshold to decide how to connect the line segments.

★ Key Point 2: assemble the case index with bitwise operations","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"203":{"title":"4.5 Marching Squares (Contour Lines)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"code","text":"//  16 种 case 每种对应 0/1/2 条线段（用边的编号表示）
// 边编号：0=左中, 1=下中, 2=右中, 3=上中
const EDGE_TABLE = [
  [],                  // 0000 全小
  [[3, 0]],            // 0001 仅 BL 大
  [[0, 1]],            // 0010 仅 BR 大
  [[3, 1]],            // 0011 BL+BR
  [[1, 2]],            // 0100 仅 TR 大
  [[3, 0], [1, 2]],    // 0101（saddle 鞍点，需）
  [[0, 2]],            // 0110 BR+TR
  [[3, 2]],            // 0111
  [[2, 3]],            // 1000 仅 TL 大
  [[2, 0]],            // 1001 TL+BL
  [[0, 1], [2, 3]],    // 1010（saddle）
  [[2, 1]],
  [[1, 3]],
  [[1, 0]],
  [[0, 3]],
  []                   // 1111 全大
];

// 4 个角是否 > 阈值 → 4 位二进制 → case 编号
let code = 0;
if (bl > threshold) code |= 1;   // bit 0
if (br > threshold) code |= 2;   // bit 1
if (tr > threshold) code |= 4;   // bit 2
if (tl > threshold) code |= 8;   // bit 3

const lines = EDGE_TABLE[code];   // 查表","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-5","query":{"tab-5":"code"}}},"204":{"title":"4.5 Marching Squares (Contour Lines)","breadcrumb":"Ch 4 · Low-Dim","kind":"demo","text":"Given a scalar field + threshold, trace the curve where value = threshold.","target":{"path":"/lessons/04","hash":"#ch-4-5","query":{"tab-5":"demo"}}},"205":{"title":"5.1 Time Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"explain","text":"Time has 5 core properties: ordered / continuous / cyclic / independent of space / structural. Each property shapes visualization design.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"206":{"title":"5.1 Time Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"explain","text":"Original section 6.1 listed the 5 time properties without any visualization. This chapter turns those 5 properties into interactive cards.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"207":{"title":"5.1 Time Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"explain","text":"Order: Time is ordered—any two events have a before/after, tightly linked to causality.
Continuity: Time is continuous—between any two moments there is always another moment.
Periodicity: Natural processes often follow cyclic patterns (seasons, day/night)—a cyclic time domain can be used.
Independence from space: Time is tightly coupled with space, but scientific processes usually treat them independently.
Structure: Time scales split into year / month / day / hour / minute / second—some divisions reflect nature (day/night), others are human convention (60 seconds).","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"208":{"title":"5.1 Time Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"explain","text":"Thinking through these 5 properties before designing a time series chart avoids common pitfalls:
Missing order → scatter plot misaligned into noise
Confused continuity → discrete data wrongly connected
Ignored periodicity → seasonal trends “flattened out”
Mismatched structure → weekdays/weekends, leap years all go wrong","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-1","query":{"tab-1":"explain"}}},"209":{"title":"5.1 Time Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"code","text":"Time has 5 core properties: ordered / continuous / cyclic / independent of space / structural. Each property shapes visualization design.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"210":{"title":"5.1 Time Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"code","text":"Key Point 1: the 5 properties are organized as an array of objects
Array of objects = one data source, many uses. Card rendering, detailed explanations, and filtering all derive from the same source—so you never miss a spot when editing.

Key Point 2: d3.timeDay.range generates an equally-spaced day sequence
d3.timeDay is the core of the d3-time module: range(start, stop) returns a discrete array of every “day” between two moments; offset(date, n) adds or subtracts n days. Structure (structural) is achieved through this kind of “time-scale” operation.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"211":{"title":"5.1 Time Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"code","text":"//  单一数据源：5 个属性 + 含义 + 例子 + 颜色
const TIME_ATTRIBUTES = [
  { key: 'ordered',          name: '有序性',     icon: '',
    desc: '时间是有序的。两个事件发生的时间有先后次序，与因果关系紧密相连。',
    example: '火车时刻表、事件日志、git commit history', color: '#3b82f6' },
  { key: 'continuous',       name: '连续性',     icon: '∞',
    desc: '时间是连续的。两个时间点之间总存在另一个时间点。',
    example: '实时传感器数据（温度、心率）、流式数据流', color: '#10b981' },
  { key: 'periodic',         name: '周期性',     icon: '',
    desc: '许多自然过程有循环规律（季节、昼夜）。可采用循环时间域表示。',
    example: '24h 体温、12 个月气温、年周期销售', color: '#f59e0b' },
  { key: 'spatial_independent', name: '独立于空间', icon: '',
    desc: '时间与空间紧相关，但科学过程大多将它们独立处理。',
    example: '折线图 x 轴只用时间、y 轴只用度量', color: '#8b5cf6' },
  { key: 'structured',       name: '结构性',     icon: '',
    desc: '时间尺度分年/月/日/时/分/秒，分割既有自然反映（昼夜）也有人为定义。',
    example: '日历/周排列、闰年调整、工作日 vs 周末', color: '#ec4899' }
];

// 生成 2024-01-01 到 2024-12-31 之间所有日期（366 天）
const days = d3.timeDay.range(new Date(2024, 0, 1), new Date(2025, 0, 1));
// 输出：[Date(2024-01-01), Date(2024-01-02), ..., Date(2024-12-31)]
console.log(days.length);  // 366（闰年）","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-1","query":{"tab-1":"code"}}},"212":{"title":"5.1 Time Attributes","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"demo","text":"Time has 5 core properties: ordered / continuous / cyclic / independent of space / structural. Each property shapes visualization design.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-1","query":{"tab-1":"demo"}}},"213":{"title":"5.2 Standard Time Viz (Line Chart)","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"explain","text":"x-axis = time, y-axis = measure. The most common time series chart: frequency of French names from 1900–2020.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"214":{"title":"5.2 Standard Time Viz (Line Chart)","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"explain","text":"Original section 6.2.1 used line charts to show usage-frequency changes of French names (Marie / Jean / Camille) over decades. The original page bundled “word cloud + hover highlight + time-range filter,” but the code only included the key line segments.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"215":{"title":"5.2 Standard Time Viz (Line Chart)","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"explain","text":"Essence of the standard time series chart: a 2D line chart with x = time, y = measure. The advantage is that change trends in a linear time domain are immediately clear.
Drawback: hard to express periodicity—hence section 5.3 calendar heatmap and section 5.4 spiral.
Interaction: hover highlight, tooltip, time-range filtering—the data-journalism standard.
Choosing in practice: ≤50 points use linear; 50–500 use monotoneX / curveCatmullRom; >500 consider area / box plot.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-2","query":{"tab-2":"explain"}}},"216":{"title":"5.2 Standard Time Viz (Line Chart)","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"code","text":"x-axis = time, y-axis = measure. The most common time series chart: frequency of French names from 1900–2020.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"217":{"title":"5.2 Standard Time Viz (Line Chart)","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"code","text":"Key Point 1: d3.timeParse + d3.timeFormat for time formatting
d3-time is a core d3 module. d3.timeParse / timeFormat must be mastered first when handling any time series data—especially since CSV years are strings like “2024,” not Date objects.

Key Point 2: d3.scaleTime time axis
scaleTime has two extra capabilities over scaleLinear: 1) auto-pick tick intervals based on time span (10 years / 12 months / 30 days); 2) timezone handling (in real projects, remember to set utc()).

Key Point 3: hover highlight + tooltip
Generic hover template: transparent rect + focus g (line + circle + text). d3.bisector finds the nearest point faster and more accurately than a hand-written loop.","target":{"path":"/lessons/05","hash":"#ch-5-2","query":{"tab-2":"code"}}},"218":{"title":"5.2 Standard Time Viz (Line Chart)","breadcrumb":"Ch 5 · Time Series","kind":"code","text":"//  时间字符串 → Date 对象
const parseYear = d3.timeParse('%Y');        // '1965' → Date
// 时间 Date → 字符串
const formatYear = d3.timeFormat('%Y');     // Date → '1965'

// 典型 timeFormat tokens
//   %Y 4 位年    %y 2 位年
//   %m 月份 01-12   %B 月名 January
//   %d 日 01-31    %a 星期 Sun
//   %H 时 00-23    %M 分 00-59
//   %L 毫秒 000-999

// 时间轴 = 数值轴的特化版（Date 在底层就是数字）
const xScale = d3.scaleTime()
  .domain([new Date(1900, 0, 1), new Date(2020, 11, 31)])
  .range([0, innerW]);
// d3.axisBottom 自动按"年/十年/世纪"切换 tick
svg.append('g')
  .attr('transform', `translate(${M.l},${M.t + innerH})`)
  .call(d3.axisBottom(xScale).ticks(10));

// 透明覆盖层捕获鼠标事件
const focus = svg.append('g').style('display', 'none');
focus.append('line')             // 垂直参考线
  .attr('class', 'hover-line')
  .attr('y1', 0).attr('y2', innerH);
focus.append('circle')           // 圆点
